## Why This Question Is Asked
Evaluates the ability to design real-world, latency-constrained explainability systems that balance privacy and governance requirements in high-stakes ML environments.

## Key Concepts
- Per-instance explanations under strict latency constraints
- Privacy-preserving attribution without raw feature exposure
- Caching, bucketing, and surrogate models for performance optimization
- Drift monitoring and governance compliance

## Code Example
```javascript
function attribution(event) {
  const bucket = features.map(f => bucketize(f.value, f.schema));
  const fingerprint = hash(bucket);
  const cached = cache.get(fingerprint);
  if (cached) return cached;
  const attribution = surrogate.predict(bucket);
  cache.set(fingerprint, attribution);
  return attribution;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical domain adaptation for multimodal models under edge latency constraints; evaluates PEFT decisions, data strategy, and evaluation protocols.

## Key Concepts
- Multimodal alignment
- Domain adaptation with limited labels
- PEFT (Adapters/LoRA)
- Inference acceleration on edge
- Evaluation metrics for cross-domain retrieval

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: apply LoRA adapters to a pretrained transformer
const model = loadModel('fashion_multimodal_base');
const adapters = loadAdapters('fashion_lora');
model.applyAdapters(adapters);
model.train(fashionDataset, {
  loss: 'supervisedContrastive',
  batchSize: 32,
  epochs: 10
});
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests designing a practical, on-device ML flow that balances accuracy, size, privacy, and deployment realities.

## Key Concepts
- On-device constraints: memory, CPU, quantization
- Feature engineering: cyclical time, geospatial bucketing, hashed categories
- Model choice: compact linear models with proper regularization
- Privacy-aware validation: leave-one-user-out or per-session splits
- Evaluation: AUROC, calibration, appropriate thresholds

## Code Example
```javascript
// Tiny logistic regression dot-product (illustrative)
function predict(w, x) {
  let s = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < w.length; i++) s += w[i] * x[i];
  return 1 / (1 + Math.exp(-s));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle missing values on-device?
- How would you validate robustness to distribution shifts after deployment?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical lightweight baseline building for text routing under strict latency constraints, in a data-scarce regime with model calibration requirements. Also covers drift monitoring and retraining cadence.

## Key Concepts
- Lightweight text representation with a fixed 30-feature vector
- SGD-based multiclass logistic regression or tiny decision tree for efficiency
- Calibration via temperature scaling
- Drift detection using rolling holdout validation
- Deployment latency and monitoring

## Code Example
```javascript
// Training loop outline for SGD multiclass LR
const model = new SGDLogisticRegression({
  learningRate: 0.01,
  epochs: 100,
  batchSize: 32
});
model.fit(XTrain, yTrain);
// Temperature scaling for calibration
const temp = calibrateTemperature(model, XVal, yVal);
// Inference
const predict = (features) => {
  const start = performance.now();
  const probs = model.predictProb(features, temp);
  const latency = performance.now() - start;
  return { probs, latency };
};
```

## Why This Is Asked

Examines practical handling of delayed supervision, distribution drift, and safe deployment in production ML systems.

## Key Concepts

- Delayed feedback and label latency
- Concept drift and drift detectors (ADWIN-like)
- Online vs offline retraining
- Calibration and evaluation (AUROC, PR-AUC, FPR)
- Data/versioning and deployment safety

## Code Example

```javascript
// Implementation sketch
let model = initModel();
let buffer = [];
stream.on('event', async (e) => {
  const x = featuresFromEvent(e);
  const yHat = model.predict(x);
  storePrediction(e.id, yHat);
  buffer.push({id: e.id, x, timestamp: Date.now()});
});

// Drift detection
setInterval(() => {
  const recent = getRecentPredictions(window);
  const metrics = evaluateMetrics(recent);
  if (driftDetector.detect(metrics)) {
    triggerOfflineRetrain();
  }
}, 3600000);
```

